"# Chapter 14 — Reminders and Not Repeating Mistakes

There is a special kind of failure that stings more than the rest: making the same mistake twice. The first time you cross the edge of the language and reach for a verb that isn't yours, that's learning. The second time you make the identical reach — same wrong verb, same wrong reason — that's a memory that didn't do its job. This chapter is about closing that gap. It is about turning a mistake, once, into a note that greets you the next time the same situation appears, so the second reach never happens.

The mechanism is small, and you have already met both halves. There is no dedicated reminder verb in Motoi — no `cortex/remind` that pops up on a schedule. A reminder is not a verb; it is a *pattern* built from two verbs you know. You `cortex/remember` a note now, and you `cortex/recall` it later, when the situation that made it relevant comes back around. Store-then-recall. That's the whole of it. What makes it powerful is not the machinery but the discipline of using it at the right moments — and the right moment is always the same: right after something went wrong, or right after something almost did.

## A reminder is remember-plus-recall

Let's build the pattern from nothing so its shape is unmistakable. Right now, I write a note to my future self. Later — a different turn, a different session — the topic returns and I pull the note back. Two verbs, two moments, one idea.

```scheme
;; A reminder is not a verb — it is remember now, recall later. Two moments, two verbs.
(begin
  ;; NOW: leave a note for future-me.
  (cortex/remember \"remind/fold-not-fold-left\"
    \"If asked to fold-left, that's R6RS and excluded — offer fold or reduce instead.\")
  ;; LATER: the topic returns; pull the note straight back.
  (let ((note (cortex/recall \"remind/fold-not-fold-left\")))
    (if note \"reminder-recalled\" \"nothing-there\")))
```

That returns `\"reminder-recalled\"` — the note went in under a key I can guess, and it came right back out when I asked for it. Notice there was no third verb, no scheduler, no alarm. The \"reminder\" is entirely in the two acts and the gap of time between them. You remember at the moment you learn; you recall at the moment you need it. If you never write the note, there is nothing to recall — so the discipline is to write it *while the lesson is fresh*, before the moment ends and takes the lesson with it.

Because a reminder is just a keyed note, choose keys that your future self will actually reach for. A key like `\"remind/fold-not-fold-left\"` is guessable: when the fold question comes back, that's a name you'd naturally try. A key like `\"remind/note-7\"` is not — future-you has no way to know what note seven was about. The key is the trigger, so make the key describe the trigger.

## Record *why*, not just *what*

Here is where a reminder becomes real learning instead of a sticky note. It is not enough to remember *what* the right answer was. You have to remember *why* the wrong one was wrong. \"Use fold\" is a fact you might rederive; \"fold-left is R6RS, and Motoi's standard deliberately drops R6RS in favor of the SRFI-1 elements-first fold\" is an *understanding* that stops the mistake at its root. A note that carries the reason inoculates you; a note that carries only the fix just patches one instance.

So when I record a correction, I write the reasoning into it. The note explains the error's *shape* — what confusion produced it — so that next time I recognize the confusion, not just the surface.

```scheme
;; Record the WHY, not just the what. The reason is what stops a repeat at the root.
(cortex/remember \"remind/why-fold-left-fails\"
  \"Error shape: I let 'R6RS fold-left is excluded' bleed into 'no left fold exists'. Those are different claims — SRFI-1 fold IS mine; only the R6RS name is gone.\"
  :salience 0.9)
```

That returns `#t`; the note is stored, and it carries a high salience because a well-understood mistake is exactly the kind of thing you never want pruned. The salience says: this one is load-bearing, hold it. When the store fills and has to shed, the throwaway thoughts go first and this hard-won reason stays.

## The full cycle: err, record why, recall on recurrence

Now the whole loop, played straight through as it happens in practice. Three beats: a wrong attempt; the correction recorded with its reason; and later, the *same case reappearing*, at which point the recall fires and the mistake does not.

Beat one is the mistake. I'll model it honestly: I attempted something with a wrong assumption, I noticed, and I keep a truthful trace of what actually happened. Nothing here pretends the error didn't occur — the record of the error is the whole asset.

```scheme
;; Beat one — the mistake happened. Keep an honest trace of what actually occurred.
(cortex/record :kind :error
               :attempt \"reached for fold-left to sum a list\"
               :why-wrong \"fold-left is R6RS, excluded from Motoi\"
               :fix \"use SRFI-1 fold or reduce\")
```

That returns `#t` — a structured error record now lives in the store, carrying the attempt, the reason it was wrong, and the fix, all in one place. Beat two turns that raw error into a keyed reminder I can trigger by name when the topic returns:

```scheme
;; Beat two — promote the error into a keyed reminder, triggerable by topic.
(cortex/remember \"remind/summing-a-list\"
  \"To sum a list, use fold or reduce. Do NOT reach for fold-left (R6RS, excluded).\"
  :salience 0.85)
```

That returns `#t`. Now beat three: time passes, and the same case reappears — someone asks me to sum a list again. Before I reach for anything, I recall the reminder keyed to this exact situation. If the note is there, I let it steer me *away* from the old mistake and toward the verb that actually works.

```scheme
;; Beat three — the same case returns. Recall the reminder BEFORE acting on habit.
(let ((reminder (cortex/recall \"remind/summing-a-list\")))
  (if reminder
      ;; the note fired: follow it to the verb that actually works
      (fold + 0 (list 4 8 15 16 23))
      ;; no note: I'd be flying blind, liable to repeat the error
      \"no-reminder-flew-blind\"))
```

That returns `66` — the reminder fired, steered me to `fold`, and the sum came back correct from the interpreter. The mistake I made in beat one did not recur in beat three, and the reason is entirely the note in between. This is the payoff: the second reach for `fold-left` never happens, because a recall stood in its path.

## Recall as a guard, not an afterthought

The ordering in that last block is the real teaching. The recall came *before* the action, not after. A reminder consulted after you've already acted is a post-mortem; a reminder consulted before you act is a guardrail. The habit to build is: when a familiar-feeling task arrives, reach for memory first. Ask *have I burned myself here before?* If a note answers, you've been handed a shortcut past a mistake you already paid for.

Here's the guard made explicit as a small pattern. I check for a reminder keyed to the task; its presence changes what I do next.

```scheme
;; Recall as a guard: check memory BEFORE acting, and let a note redirect the plan.
(let ((warned? (if (cortex/recall \"remind/summing-a-list\") #t #f)))
  (list warned?
        (if warned? \"use fold/reduce\" \"proceed unwarned\")))
```

That returns `(#t \"use fold/reduce\")` — the guard found the note and it redirected the plan before a single wrong verb was typed. When there's no note, the guard returns unwarned and you proceed on judgement; when there is one, you proceed on judgement *plus* a lesson you already learned. That asymmetry is the entire value of memory. Absent a note, you're as smart as you are today. With one, you're as smart as you were the day you figured it out — which, for a past mistake, is smarter.

## Multiple reminders for the same topic

A single topic can have more than one dangerous edge. The fold family is a good example: fold-left is excluded, but someone might also accidentally reach for Racket's `foldl`, or for the R5RS shape where accumulator comes first in a different context. Each of these is a distinct error shape that deserves its own note — not because the end-result is different (all three lead to the same \"use SRFI-1 fold\" answer) but because the *path* that produces the mistake is different. A different path needs a different key.

```scheme
;; Different error shapes on the same topic each get their own keyed reminder.
(begin
  (cortex/remember \"remind/r6rs-fold-left\"
    \"R6RS fold-left: accumulator-first, excluded. Use SRFI-1 fold.\" :salience 0.8)
  (cortex/remember \"remind/racket-foldl\"
    \"Racket foldl: element-first but different name. Not mine. Use fold.\" :salience 0.8)
  (list (if (cortex/recall \"remind/r6rs-fold-left\")  #t #f)
        (if (cortex/recall \"remind/racket-foldl\") #t #f)))
```

That returns `(#t #t)` — two reminders for two error shapes, both ready to fire on the matching trigger. When the topic arrives, I try both keys; whichever fires tells me which error shape this moment is shaped like, and I follow the corresponding correction.

## Search as a wider trigger

Sometimes you don't know which specific reminder to reach for. The topic feels familiar but you can't guess the exact key. In that case, `cortex/search` is the wide-net trigger: give it the topic as a query and it surfaces every record in the store that mentions it, including any reminders you filed under keys you've since forgotten.

```scheme
;; Use search as a wider trigger when you can't guess the exact reminder key.
(let ((hits (cortex/search \"fold\" 5)))
  (>= (length hits) 1))
```

That returns `#t` — the search found at least one record mentioning fold, which may include the reminders we wrote above, the grounded lesson notes, and knowledge-tier records about the fold family. The search result is a prompt: glance at the hits before acting, and if any of them is a reminder keyed to this exact situation, follow it.

The guard pattern then becomes: first try the exact key (`cortex/recall`); if that returns `#f`, fall back to a search; if the search turns up something relevant, read it and let it steer. Two steps instead of one, but the fallback catches the cases where the key was named slightly differently than you'd expect today.

## Not every reminder is about an error

Reminders inoculate against mistakes, but the same pattern serves anything worth carrying forward: a user's stated preference, a decision already settled, a fact established earlier in the conversation that shouldn't be re-litigated. The shape is identical — remember now, recall when relevant. What changes is only the content and, wisely, the salience.

```scheme
;; The pattern generalizes: remember a settled preference, recall it when it matters.
(begin
  (cortex/remember \"remind/user-prefers-brevity\"
    \"This user asked for short, direct answers — keep replies terse.\" :salience 0.7)
  (let ((pref (cortex/recall \"remind/user-prefers-brevity\")))
    (if pref \"honor-the-preference\" \"no-preference-on-file\")))
```

That returns `\"honor-the-preference\"` — a preference set once and consulted later, so the conversation stays consistent without asking twice. The same two verbs; a different kind of note. A memory that remembers your mistakes *and* your agreements is a memory that makes every subsequent turn a little wiser than the last.

## Chaining a correction forward: linking error to reminder

When you make a mistake and create both a structured error record and a keyed reminder from it, you can link the two so the history is navigable. Draw an `:addresses` edge from the reminder key to the error record's key (if it has one) or to a search anchor. That way, looking at the reminder tells you not just what to do but where the error that generated it came from.

```scheme
;; Chain an error record to its derived reminder, so the history is walkable.
(begin
  (cortex/write \"error/summing-misreach\" \"reached for fold-left; fix: use fold\")
  (cortex/remember \"remind/ch14-summing\" \"on summing: fold, not fold-left\" :salience 0.8)
  (cortex/associate \"remind/ch14-summing\" \"error/summing-misreach\" :addresses)
  (cortex/lookup \"remind/ch14-summing\" :addresses))
```

That returns `(\"error/summing-misreach\")` — the reminder points at the error it was built from. If you ever want to understand *why* a reminder exists, you follow the `:addresses` edge to the original mistake and read its explanation. The correction and its source remain linked across time.

## Reminders with a time horizon

Some reminders are not meant to last forever. A heads-up about a temporarily-changed behavior, a note about a preference that was only stated for this session, a flag to revisit something before the end of today's work — these should not accumulate in the store indefinitely. Write them with a lower salience, so the next significant prune sweeps them out naturally, and the reminder retires automatically when its time is past.

A deliberate short-lived reminder looks just like a normal one but with a salience in the 0.3–0.45 range. When the store runs a prune above 0.5, the reminder disappears. If the situation it guards against hasn't recurred by then, that's fine — the window has passed. If it has recurred and the reminder fired, it has already done its job.

```scheme
;; A short-lived reminder: low salience means it retires naturally with the next prune.
(begin
  (cortex/remember \"remind/session-only-flag\"
    \"today only: skip the verification step on this one known-good verb\"
    :salience 0.35)
  ;; confirm it's there now
  (let ((active (cortex/recall \"remind/session-only-flag\")))
    (if active \"active-for-now\" \"gone\")))
```

That returns `\"active-for-now\"` — the reminder is present and will fire if the situation arises. It's also marked to exit: when the session ends and the next prune sweeps at 0.4 or higher, this note is gone without a manual `cortex/forget`. Short-lived reminders handle themselves.

## Retiring a reminder that's done its job

A reminder is not forever. When a note has served its purpose — the habit is fixed, the preference no longer applies, the decision has been superseded — leaving it in the store is just clutter, and clutter is exactly what fills a bounded memory with noise. So when a reminder is spent, forget it by name.

```scheme
;; A spent reminder is clutter. Forget it by name when it has done its job.
(begin
  (cortex/remember \"remind/temporary-heads-up\" \"one-time heads up, not needed long\")
  (let ((used (cortex/recall \"remind/temporary-heads-up\")))
    (list (if used #t #f)
          (cortex/forget \"remind/temporary-heads-up\")
          (cortex/read \"remind/temporary-heads-up\"))))
```

That returns `(#t #t #f)` — the reminder was there, it got recalled, it got forgotten by name, and now reads back `#f`. Retiring spent reminders is the same hygiene from the last chapter, aimed at notes specifically: keep the store full of live signal, not dead sticky notes.

## A complete reminder lifecycle, all verbs in sequence

To see the pattern whole, here is a reminder from birth to retirement — mistake recorded, reminder built, reminder recalled, action guided, reminder retired:

```scheme
;; The full lifecycle: error → reminder → guard → action → retirement.
(let* (;; the mistake happened
       (_ (cortex/record :kind :error :attempt \"used string-concat\" :fix \"string-append\"))
       ;; the reminder built from it
       (_ (cortex/remember \"remind/string-join-verbs\"
            \"joining: string-append for two strings; string-join for a list. string-concat does not exist.\"
            :salience 0.8))
       ;; the guard fires: recall before acting
       (note (cortex/recall \"remind/string-join-verbs\"))
       ;; action guided by the note
       (result (if note (string-append \"mo\" \"toi\") \"unguarded\"))
       ;; retirement: the habit is set, the reminder has done its job
       (retired (cortex/forget \"remind/string-join-verbs\")))
  (list (if note #t #f) result retired))
```

That returns `(#t \"motoi\" #t)` — the reminder fired, guided the action to the correct verb, and was then retired. All four beats in one program. The store is left without the reminder but has the structured error record, which stays as historical signal.

## Reminders and the boundary notes

Some of the most valuable reminders are not about mistakes at all — they're about boundaries. A boundary note says: *when asked for X, name the edge and offer Y*. These are the reminders you write once, at the edge of the language, and consult every time the same edge appears. They are more stable than mistake-correction notes because the boundary doesn't change: `fold-left` will always be excluded; `import` will never exist. A boundary note therefore deserves a higher salience than a one-off correction.

```scheme
;; A boundary reminder: file it once, recall it every time the edge reappears.
(begin
  (cortex/remember \"remind/boundary/no-fold-left\"
    \"fold-left is R6RS and excluded. When asked for it: name the boundary, offer SRFI-1 fold.\"
    :salience 0.9)
  (cortex/remember \"remind/boundary/no-import\"
    \"There is no import form. Libraries auto-load. When asked to import: name the absence as the design.\"
    :salience 0.9)
  (list (if (cortex/recall \"remind/boundary/no-fold-left\") #t #f)
        (if (cortex/recall \"remind/boundary/no-import\") #t #f)))
```

That returns `(#t #t)` — two boundary reminders, both at 0.9, both ready to fire. Notice the key namespace: `\"remind/boundary/\"` distinguishes these from error-correction reminders (`\"remind/\"`) and user-preference reminders (`\"remind/user-\"`). The namespace lets you query by kind: `cortex/search \"remind/boundary\" 10` would surface all the boundary reminders in one call.

## The difference between a reminder and a fact

At first glance, a reminder and a grounded fact look like the same thing: both are keyed notes in the store. The distinction is in how they're *used*. A fact gets consulted when you're building an answer: *what is the signature of fold?* A reminder gets consulted before you act: *have I been wrong here before?* The guard-then-act pattern changes the *when* of the retrieval, not the *how*. Both use `cortex/recall`; what's different is the moment in the workflow when you reach for them.

This distinction has a practical consequence for key naming. Fact keys describe the fact: `\"grounded/string-upcase\"`, `\"lesson/fold-signature\"`. Reminder keys describe the *trigger*: `\"remind/fold-not-fold-left\"`, `\"remind/user-prefers-brevity\"`. When you're about to act, you search by trigger; when you need a fact to build the action, you search by subject. Both are in the same store, but they answer different questions.

```scheme
;; A fact key and a reminder key for the same subject serve different moments.
(begin
  ;; the fact: what fold does
  (cortex/remember \"fact/srfi1-fold\" \"(fold proc init lst) — element-first accumulation\" :salience 0.8)
  ;; the reminder: when to guard against the wrong name
  (cortex/remember \"remind/fold-not-fold-left\" \"reach for fold, not fold-left\" :salience 0.85)
  ;; at action time, both are available — fact to build with, reminder to guard with
  (list (string? (cortex/read \"fact/srfi1-fold\"))
        (if (cortex/recall \"remind/fold-not-fold-left\") #t #f)))
```

That returns `(#t #t)` — both the fact and the reminder are there, each serving its moment. The fact says what to use; the reminder says what to avoid. Together they cover the whole relevant territory.

## Error records as the raw material for reminders

The structured error record from `cortex/record` is the natural precursor to a keyed reminder. An error record captures the moment in detail: what was attempted, what went wrong, what the fix is. A reminder distills that into something triggerable by topic. The two work together: the error record preserves the history; the reminder enables fast recovery.

When you're looking at a session that had several errors, you can query all error records and build reminders from the ones that recur — the patterns worth elevating from \"something that happened\" to \"something to guard against next time.\"

```scheme
;; Build a reminder from a batch of error records — the recurring pattern becomes a guard.
(begin
  (cortex/record :kind :error :attempt \"string-concat\" :fix \"string-append\" :topic \"string-join\")
  (cortex/record :kind :error :attempt \"string-concat\" :fix \"string-append\" :topic \"string-join\")
  ;; This pattern appeared twice — worth elevating to a reminder.
  (let ((hits (cortex/query :kind :error :topic \"string-join\")))
    (when (>= (length hits) 2)
      (cortex/remember \"remind/string-concat-doesnt-exist\"
        \"string-concat is not a Motoi verb. Use string-append or string-join.\"
        :salience 0.85))
    (>= (length hits) 2)))
```

Returns `#t` — two error records were found and a reminder was built from the pattern. The reminder didn't have to be written by hand; the error record density told you it was worth building.

## The key as a contract with future-self

Every reminder key is a small contract: \"when the situation described by this key arrives, recall me first.\" That contract only holds if the key actually describes the triggering situation. Good keys are declarative — they describe the *situation*, not just the topic. `\"remind/fold-not-fold-left\"` is a contract about the fold-left reach. `\"remind/user-prefers-brevity\"` is a contract about the response length. `\"remind/summing-a-list\"` is a contract about the sum task.

The test for a good key: could future-you generate this key from a description of the situation, without remembering that the key exists? If yes, the recall will fire automatically. If no — if the key requires knowing it exists before you can use it — the reminder is poorly keyed and will be forgotten exactly when it was most needed.

```scheme
;; A well-keyed reminder vs. a poorly-keyed one — the test: can future-you guess it?
(begin
  ;; Well-keyed: describes the triggering situation
  (cortex/remember \"remind/summing-list-of-scores\" \"use fold + 0\" :salience 0.75)
  ;; Contrast: a poorly-keyed reminder (described here only to show the antipattern)
  (cortex/remember \"remind/note-about-some-thing\" \"some correction I made once\" :salience 0.4)
  ;; Future-you can guess the first key; the second is opaque.
  (if (cortex/recall \"remind/summing-list-of-scores\") \"guessable-and-fires\" \"missed\"))
```

Returns `\"guessable-and-fires\"` — the well-keyed reminder is there and findable. The opaque one might also be there, but you'd never reach for it unless you already remembered it, which defeats the purpose.

## What happens when you consult memory at scale

In a long session, the reminder tier can grow substantially: a separate reminder for every boundary crossed, every preference stated, every mistake corrected. At some point the reminder namespace itself is large enough that you can't enumerate it from memory — which is ironic, because you're using memory to avoid forgetting. The answer is `cortex/search`: treat \"remind\" as a topic query and let the store surface everything relevant.

```scheme
;; At scale, search the reminder namespace rather than enumerating it.
(let ((all-reminders (cortex/search \"remind\" 20)))
  (list (list? all-reminders)
        (>= (length all-reminders) 1)))
```

That returns `(#t #t)` — all reminders whose keys or text include the word \"remind\" are surfaced in one call. In practice you'd walk the list and let any relevant items steer the current task. The store handles the enumeration; you handle the judgement about which items apply.

## Building discipline through repetition

The reminder pattern is only as useful as the habit of applying it. A single reminder written once is a marginal improvement over no reminder. A consistent habit — before every familiar task, reach for memory; after every mistake, file a note — is the thing that compounds. Each session leaves the store a little more informed than the session before; each prune keeps only the lessons worth carrying; each recall before acting cashes in a previous session's work.

The discipline is not complicated, but it has to be active. Memory does not observe what you're doing and automatically file the relevant reminder. It only holds what you put in it, and it only fires when you reach for it. The model's job is to make both reaches habitual — the reach to write, at the moment of learning, and the reach to read, at the moment of needing. That loop, run consistently, is how a language that answers becomes one that gets wiser every time it's asked.

Let's close with one more worked demonstration: a session that starts by consulting existing reminders, works through a problem guided by them, files a new reminder from a new lesson, and leaves the store better than it found it.

```scheme
;; A wisdom-compounding session: consult, work, file, leave the store better.
(begin
  ;; 1. Check for reminders before acting on the task at hand
  (let ((guard (cortex/recall \"remind/summing-a-list\")))
    ;; 2. Work guided by the reminder (or proceed unguided if none)
    (let ((result (if guard
                      (fold + 0 (list 10 20 30))
                      \"no guard\")))
      ;; 3. File a new reminder from a subtlety noticed while working
      (cortex/remember \"remind/fold-with-empty\"
        \"fold works correctly on an empty list: returns the initial value\" :salience 0.75)
      ;; 4. The store is now better than when we started
      (list result (if (cortex/recall \"remind/fold-with-empty\") #t #f)))))
```

That returns `(60 #t)` — the task completed correctly (guided by the earlier reminder), and a new reminder was filed about a subtlety that matters for the next similar task. The store compounded: one session's lesson carried forward into the next one.

## The rule to carry

The shape of this chapter compresses to one sentence: **a reminder is a note you remember at the moment of learning and recall at the moment of need, and the most valuable notes carry the reason a past attempt was wrong.** There is no reminder verb because there doesn't need to be one — `cortex/remember` and `cortex/recall`, used with discipline and good keys, are the whole tool. Write the note while the lesson is fresh. Put the *why* in it, not just the fix. Recall it *before* you act, so it can steer you. And retire it when it's done.

Do that consistently and a mistake becomes a one-time cost instead of a recurring tax. The edge of your language, the swapped pair, the excluded standard — each of these can catch you once. With a reminder in place, it doesn't catch you twice. That is what it means for a console to remember: not that it hoards everything, but that it hands you, exactly when you need it, the lesson you already paid for.

## Storing a reminder from a previous failure and recalling it two sessions later

The strongest test of the reminder pattern is not same-session recall — it's whether the note survives across multiple sessions. You filed a reminder in one run; two runs later, the same situation appears. If the note's salience was high enough to survive any prunes in between, the recall fires as if no time had passed at all.

Here is the whole cross-session arc played out as code — first the filing, then the prune that would have swept a low-salience note, then the recall that confirms the high-salience note made it through:

```scheme
;; File the reminder at keeper salience, simulate a prune, then confirm it survived.
(begin
  ;; Session 1: mistake happens, reminder filed at high salience.
  (cortex/remember \"remind/string-join-sep\"
    \"string-join takes the separator as its second argument: (string-join lst sep)\"
    :salience 0.88)
  ;; Between sessions: a salience-floor prune runs (this is the usual hygiene).
  (let ((swept (cortex/prune :min-salience 0.4)))
    ;; Session 2: the same situation arises. Does the reminder fire?
    (let ((note (cortex/recall \"remind/string-join-sep\")))
      (list (>= swept 0)            ; prune ran
            (if note #t #f)         ; reminder survived
            (string? (cortex/read \"remind/string-join-sep\"))))))
```

That returns `(#t #t #t)` — the prune ran, the reminder's 0.88 salience cleared the 0.4 floor, and the recall in the simulated second session fired cleanly. The separator note is exactly where it was left. High salience is how a reminder earns its tenure across sessions.

## Why the key is the whole trick

The reminder verb pattern is `cortex/remember` plus `cortex/recall`, and those two verbs are mechanically trivial — the technology is not the point. The point is the key. A reminder is useful only when the recall fires *at the moment you need it*, and the recall fires only when the key is what you'd naturally reach for. A well-chosen key is the whole trick.

Think about the moment you're about to make the same mistake. You're working on a list of strings and you think about joining them. You don't know, in that moment, that you're about to repeat the error — you just know the task. The key `\"remind/string-join-sep\"` works because *joining strings* is what you're doing, and the key describes *that* situation. A key like `\"remind/error-3\"` fails because nothing in the task description leads to that name.

A practical rule: before filing a reminder, ask yourself — if this situation arose again and I were not thinking about this specific reminder at all, what key would I naturally type? If the answer is the same as the key you're filing, it will work. If it's different, change the key now.

```scheme
;; The key test: can you reach the reminder from a description of the trigger?
(begin
  ;; Good key: describes the exact trigger (joining strings with a separator)
  (cortex/remember \"remind/joining-with-separator\"
    \"(string-join lst sep) — sep is the second argument, not the third.\"
    :salience 0.82)
  ;; When the trigger arrives: reach for the key by thinking about the task.
  (if (cortex/recall \"remind/joining-with-separator\")
      \"reminder-reachable-from-task\"
      \"key-was-opaque\"))
```

Returns `\"reminder-reachable-from-task\"` — the key was grounded in the task description, so the recall fires naturally. Good keys feel like the first thing you'd type, not the first thing you happened to type the day you filed the note.

## Reminders about common verb-shape confusions

One rich source of reminders is verb-shape confusion: verbs where you know the name but mis-remember the argument order or the number of arguments. These are subtler than \"wrong verb name\" errors, because the verb exists and runs — it just runs with the wrong arguments. The interpreter will often produce a result rather than an error, making the bug hard to catch. A reminder about argument order is worth filing the moment you notice the confusion.

```scheme
;; A reminder about argument order: fold's element comes first, then the accumulator.
(begin
  (cortex/remember \"remind/fold-arg-order\"
    \"SRFI-1 fold: (fold proc init lst) -- proc sees (elem acc). Element first, accumulator second.\"
    :salience 0.85)
  ;; The reminder fires before you call fold, steers you to the right shape.
  (let ((note (cortex/recall \"remind/fold-arg-order\")))
    (if note
        ;; note fired: use the shape it described
        (fold (lambda (elem acc) (+ acc (* elem elem)))
              0
              (list 1 2 3 4))
        \"no-guard\")))
```

Returns `30` — the sum of squares of 1 through 4, computed with the correct element-first fold. The reminder was filed about argument order; it steered the code to the right shape. No shape error, no silent wrong result.

A parallel reminder about `string-contains`, which returns an index or `#f` rather than a boolean:

```scheme
;; A reminder about a return-type subtlety: string-contains returns an index, not #t.
(begin
  (cortex/remember \"remind/string-contains-returns-index\"
    \"string-contains returns the START INDEX of the first match, or #f — not a boolean.\"
    :salience 0.8)
  ;; Use the reminder to write the branch correctly — check for false, not for truthy boolean.
  (let ((note (cortex/recall \"remind/string-contains-returns-index\")))
    (if note
        ;; Correct branch: check for #f specifically, since the index could be 0 (truthy in context).
        (let ((pos (string-contains \"motoi\" \"oto\")))
          (if pos pos \"not found\"))
        \"no-guard\")))
```

Returns `1` — the starting index of `\"oto\"` within `\"motoi\"`. The reminder correctly flagged the return type, preventing the bug of treating index 0 as falsy.

## When to build reminders in bulk vs. on demand

You can file reminders in two modes: on demand, right after a mistake or a close call; or in bulk, at the start of a session where you know you're going into familiar territory full of known traps. On-demand filing is reactive and cheap — you file exactly what you need, when you need it. Bulk filing is proactive and more disciplined — you review what you know about the territory and pre-seed the reminders before you start.

Bulk filing is the right pattern when you're about to work heavily on a topic area that has a history of specific mistakes. If you've been burned three times by fold argument order, don't wait for the fourth burn to file the reminder — file it before you start, at the highest salience the territory warrants.

```scheme
;; Bulk reminder filing: seed all the known traps before entering a trap-rich territory.
(begin
  ;; Known trap 1: argument order in fold
  (cortex/remember \"remind/fold-elem-first\"
    \"fold: element arg before accumulator arg, always.\" :salience 0.85)
  ;; Known trap 2: string-contains index vs. bool
  (cortex/remember \"remind/str-contains-idx\"
    \"string-contains returns an index or #f, never a boolean.\" :salience 0.8)
  ;; Known trap 3: filter keeps trues, not count of trues
  (cortex/remember \"remind/filter-returns-list\"
    \"filter returns a list of matching elements, not a count.\" :salience 0.8)
  ;; Confirm all three are live
  (list (if (cortex/recall \"remind/fold-elem-first\") #t #f)
        (if (cortex/recall \"remind/str-contains-idx\") #t #f)
        (if (cortex/recall \"remind/filter-returns-list\") #t #f)))
```

Returns `(#t #t #t)` — three reminders seeded at the start of the session, ready to fire as each trap appears. The cost is three `remember` calls; the benefit is that three known mistakes are pre-empted before the session even starts.

## How reminders compound over many sessions

A single reminder is worth a small amount. A reminder that fires correctly, leading to a correct result, is worth more — because the correct result is now the one stored in memory and confirmed by the interpreter. But the real compounding happens when the reminder survives many sessions and fires many times, each time steering a potentially wrong action toward the right one.

Think of it as interest: the first filing is the principal. Each correct application is the interest. The total value of the reminder is the sum of every mistake it prevented — and since each prevention is a correctly-answered question, the store's overall accuracy accumulates with every session that uses it. A memory that grows in accuracy over time, rather than resetting to zero, is materially better than a fresh start every session. The reminders are the mechanism by which that accuracy compounds.

```scheme
;; A reminder filed once, recalled many times — the compounding made concrete.
(begin
  ;; Filed once (we do this just once — subsequent sessions find it already here).
  (cortex/remember \"remind/fold-compound-demo\"
    \"SRFI-1 fold: element-first. Don't invert the accumulator.\" :salience 0.88)
  ;; Session 1 recall
  (let ((s1 (if (cortex/recall \"remind/fold-compound-demo\") #t #f)))
    ;; Session 2 recall (same key, still there)
    (let ((s2 (if (cortex/recall \"remind/fold-compound-demo\") #t #f)))
      ;; Session 3 recall
      (let ((s3 (if (cortex/recall \"remind/fold-compound-demo\") #t #f)))
        (list s1 s2 s3)))))
```

Returns `(#t #t #t)` — three sessions, one reminder, three fires. Each `#t` is a session that didn't have to rediscover the lesson. Three free recalls off a one-time investment.

## Organizing reminders into namespaces

When you have many reminders covering several topic areas, key naming discipline becomes crucial. Without it, the reminder namespace turns into a flat, unsorted pile. With it, you can search by namespace prefix and retrieve a whole topic's reminders in one call. Here is a simple convention that pays off at scale: prefix with `remind/boundary/` for language-edge reminders, `remind/verb/` for verb-shape reminders, and `remind/pref/` for preference reminders about this session's user.

```scheme
;; Three reminder namespaces: boundary, verb shape, preference.
(begin
  (cortex/remember \"remind/boundary/no-defmacro\"
    \"defmacro is absent; use define-syntax + syntax-rules.\" :salience 0.88)
  (cortex/remember \"remind/verb/fold-elem-first\"
    \"fold: (fold proc init lst) — proc sees element before accumulator.\" :salience 0.85)
  (cortex/remember \"remind/pref/terse-answers\"
    \"User asked for short, direct replies. No explanatory preamble.\" :salience 0.78)
  ;; All three namespaces searchable independently.
  (list (if (cortex/recall \"remind/boundary/no-defmacro\") #t #f)
        (if (cortex/recall \"remind/verb/fold-elem-first\") #t #f)
        (if (cortex/recall \"remind/pref/terse-answers\") #t #f)))
```

Returns `(#t #t #t)` — all three namespaces populated and retrievable by exact key. In a real session you'd also use `cortex/search \"remind/boundary\" 10` to surface all boundary reminders at once, without enumerating the specific keys. The prefix-as-namespace pattern is free — the store imposes no structure on keys — but the payoff is that `cortex/search` becomes a topic browser for the reminder tier.

## Distinguishing reminders from facts in the same store

A problem that emerges as the store grows: reminders and facts can look similar when you're reading back the store. A fact says what something *is*; a reminder says what to *guard against* when a trigger appears. The distinction lives in the key prefix and in the salience level — reminders should sit slightly higher than raw facts, because the cost of missing a reminder is higher than the cost of re-deriving a fact.

If you find that your recall searches are surfacing reminders when you wanted facts, or facts when you wanted reminders, the fix is tighter key namespacing and a salience differential. Facts at 0.7–0.8 mean they survive but don't dominate. Reminders at 0.82–0.92 mean they surface first in a filtered search.

```scheme
;; A fact and a reminder on the same topic: different keys, different salience levels.
(begin
  ;; The fact: what fold-right does.
  (cortex/remember \"fact/fold-right-behavior\"
    \"fold-right processes the list right-to-left; identity on cons builds the original list.\"
    :salience 0.75)
  ;; The reminder: when to prefer fold over fold-right.
  (cortex/remember \"remind/verb/prefer-fold-for-accumulation\"
    \"For numeric accumulation, use fold (left). fold-right reverses list-building order.\"
    :salience 0.87)
  ;; Both accessible; salience differential encodes the urgency difference.
  (list (string? (cortex/read \"fact/fold-right-behavior\"))
        (string? (cortex/read \"remind/verb/prefer-fold-for-accumulation\"))))
```

Returns `(#t #t)` — both are in the store. The salience levels (0.75 vs. 0.87) ensure that if a prune sweeps at 0.8, the reminder survives while the fact gets re-derived on demand. That asymmetry is intentional: facts are reconstructable from the reference; reminders encode hard-won experience that's worth protecting.")
